      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the number of ordered pairs of integers \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number, we must understand the behavior of the powers of the imaginary unit \\(i\\):\n\n- \\(i^1 = i \\) \n- \\(i^2 = -1 \\)\n- \\(i^3 = -i \\)\n- \\(i^4 = 1 \\)\n- \\(i^5 = i \\)\n- And so on, exhibiting a cyclical pattern with a period of 4.\n\nRecall that \\((i^n)^m = (ij)^n\\), due to \\(i^1 = i\\) meeting \\(i^2 = -1\\), repeating for any integer \\(n\\).\n\nConsidering \\(i^x + i^y\\) evaluates to a real number:\n- We must pair \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) so that \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real. \n\nThis occurs when the values' exponents result in complex numbers' polar representation (in terms of their imaginary component which vanishes). \nGiven the trigonometric interpretation of \\(i\\):\n- \\(i^x\\) and \\(i^y\\) are individually \\(i\\),\n- Only when both exponents range within cycles with operations that return values with no imaginary component (that is, sum resulting in real).\n\nThus:\n\\[i^1 + i^1 \\rightarrow i + (-i) = 0\\]\n\\[i^2 + i^3 \\rightarrow (-1) + (-(-1)) = 0\\]\n\nRepeating this pattern for all exponents \\(1 \\le i, j \\le 100\\) fulfilling \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\). There are 100 choices for \\(x\\) and 99 choices for \\(y\\). As we have two such pairs that sum to real numbers (the 0's above)\n\n\\[ \\text{Final count} = 100 \\times 99 \\]\n\nConclusively: \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9900}\n\\]",
