      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We observe that $i^2=-1$ and $i^3=i^2(-1)=-1(-1)=1$. Thus, $i^x+i^y$ is a real number if and only if $i^x$ has the same sign as $i^y$.\nSince $1\\le x<y\\le 100$, we know $x$ and $y$ alternate between odd and even because $i^1=i$, $i^3=-1$, $i^5=i$, $i^7=-1$, and so on. In addition, we know $x<y$, so $x$ will be odd and $y$ will be even.\nCombining these facts, we see that $i^x$ can be $i$ or $-1$ and $i^y$ will always be $i$. So, the only possible values are $-1-i$ and $-1+i$.\nTo count the number of ordered pairs that satisfy this, we need to count the number of times $i$ appears among the primes $x$ times $y$ times $1$, since $x$ appears $x$ times amongst the primes in the factorization of $x^y$ and $y$ appears in the same manner in the prime factorization of $y^x$.\nWe see that for any prime $y$, $i^2=-1$ makes $i^y=-1$. So when $y$ is even, $i^y=-1$ and when $y$ is odd, $i^y=i$. On the other hand, when $x$ is even, $i^x=i$ and when $x$ is odd, $i^x=-1$.\nPutting this into our equation, we get the following scenarios:\n- If $y$ is odd, then $i^y=i$, so $i^x+i^y=i+i^x$ is real.\n- If $y$ is even, then $i^y=-1$, so $i^x+(-1)=(-1+i)+i^x$ is real.\n\nFor every prime number, there is both a factor and a multiple, so we have 99 choices for the even prime and 100 choices for the odd prime. Thus, there are $99\\times100=9900$ ordered pairs that satisfy the condition, and the answer is $\\boxed{9900}$. The answer is 9900",
