      "text": "To determine the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\leq x < y \\leq 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number, we need to explore the properties of the powers of the imaginary unit \\(i\\).\n\nThe powers of \\(i\\) cycle every four terms as follows:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^1 &= i, \\\\\ni^2 &= -1, \\\\\ni^3 &= -i, \\\\\ni^4 &= 1, \\\\\ni^5 &= i, \\\\\ni^6 &= -1, \\\\\n\\text{and so on.}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i^x + i^y\\) to be a real number, the imaginary parts must cancel each other out. We list the combinations where \\(i^x + i^y = 0\\):\n1. \\(i^1 + i^{11}\\)\n2. \\(i^2 + i^{12}\\)\n3. \\(i^3 + i^{13}\\)\n4. \\(i^4 + i^{14}\\)\n\nThus, \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) must be positions in the cycle <i>11, 12, 13, 14</i>. Specifically:\n1. \\(i^1 + i^{11} = i + (-i) = 0\\)\n2. \\(i^2 + i^{12} = -1 + 1 = 0\\)\n3. \\(i^3 + i^{13} = -i + i = 0\\)\n4. \\(i^4 + i^{14} = 1 + 1 = 0\\)\n\nHence, we need to count all valid pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\leq x < y \\leq 100\\).\n\nWe will use Python to iterate through all possible pairs and count the valid pairs where the sum \\(i^x + i^y\\) equals 0.\n```python\n# Initialize counter\ncount = 0\n\n# Iterate through all values of x and y such that 1 <= x < y <= 100\nfor x in range(1, 100):\n    for y in range(x + 1, 100):\n        if i**x + i**y == 0:\n            count += 1\n\nprint(count)\n```\n```output\nNameError: name 'i' is not defined\n```\nIt looks like there was an issue with the definition of \\(i\\). In Python, we can use the sympy library to handle complex numbers appropriately.\n\nLet's use the `sympy` library to handle the complex number \\(i\\) and count the valid pairs.\n```python\n# Import the necessary library\nfrom sympy import I\n\n# Initialize counter\ncount = 0\n\n# Iterate through all values of x and y such that 1 <= x < y <= 100\nfor x in range(1, 100):\n    for y in range(x + 1, 100):\n        if I**x + I**y == 0:\n            count += 1\n\nprint(count)\n```\n```output\n990\n```\nThe number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) with \\(1 \\leq x < y \\leq 100\\) such that \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number is \\(\\boxed{990}\\).",
